## 🔌 Connections

| LED   | Arduino Pin |
| ----- | ----------- |
| LED 1 | D3          |
| LED 2 | D5          |
| LED 3 | D6          |
| LED 4 | D9          |
| LED 5 | D10         |

For every LED:

```text
Arduino Pin → 220Ω Resistor → LED Anode (+)
LED Cathode (-) → GND
```

### ⚠️ Important

The LEDs are connected to **PWM-capable pins** because some effects use `analogWrite()` to control brightness.

On an Arduino UNO, common PWM pins are:

```text
D3, D5, D6, D9, D10, D11
```

---

## 🧠 How It Works

The program stores all LED pins inside an array:

```cpp
int leds[] = {3, 5, 6, 9, 10};
```

This allows the same code to control multiple LEDs using loops.

For example:

```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < numberOfLEDs; i++) {
    digitalWrite(leds[i], HIGH);
    delay(150);
    digitalWrite(leds[i], LOW);
}
```

The program then runs different functions for each effect.

---

## 🔆 Digital vs PWM Control

### Digital control

```cpp
digitalWrite(leds[i], HIGH);
```

The LED is either:

```text
ON
OFF
```

### PWM brightness control

```cpp
analogWrite(leds[i], brightness);
```

The brightness can be controlled from:

```text
0 → OFF
255 → Full brightness
```

This is what makes **fade, glow and breathing effects** possible.

---

## 🎛️ Changing the Speed

The normal LED effects use:

```cpp
int delayTime = 150;
```

### Faster

```cpp
int delayTime = 50;
```

### Slower

```cpp
int delayTime = 500;
```

You can experiment with different values to create your own patterns.

